# story2spec — setup and worked example

## Files

| File | Goes here | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `story2spec.md` | `.github/agents/story2spec.md` | The agent. Reading discipline, when to stop and ask, and the design-document template. |
| `db-design.md` | `.github/instructions/db-design.md` | Data-access rulebook. How schema is resolved, and how LINQ vs raw SQL vs view vs stored routine is decided. |
| `dump_schema.py` | `scripts/dump_schema.py` | Reads the connection string from `appsettings*.json`, detects the provider, writes a schema snapshot. |
| *(generated)* | `docs/db/<provider>/` | OKF v0.1 knowledge bundle — one markdown concept file per table and view. What the agent reads for objects not in the DbContext. |

---

## Step 1 — Put the files in the repo

```
your-repo/
├── .github/
│   ├── agents/
│   │   └── story2spec.md
│   └── instructions/
│       └── db-design.md
├── scripts/
│   └── dump_schema.py
└── docs/
    └── db/
        └── postgres/                    # generated: an OKF v0.1 bundle
            ├── index.md                 # lists the schemas
            ├── sales/
            │   ├── index.md
            │   ├── tables/
            │   │   ├── index.md
            │   │   ├── trades.md
            │   │   └── books.md
            │   ├── views/
            │   │   ├── index.md
            │   │   └── vw_position_valuation.md
            │   └── routines/
            │       ├── index.md
            │       └── fn_order_totals.md
            └── staging/
                └── ...
```

VS Code picks up any `.md` file in `.github/agents` as a custom agent — no special suffix needed.

## Step 2 — Install the driver

```bash
python -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate            # Windows: .venv\Scripts\activate

pip install "psycopg[binary]"        # PostgreSQL
pip install pyodbc                   # SQL Server — also needs MS ODBC Driver 18 at OS level
pip install oracledb                 # Oracle
```

Only the driver for the databases you actually dump. The script itself uses only the standard library.

## Step 3 — Check what the script sees, before connecting

```bash
python scripts/dump_schema.py --list
```

Output looks like:

```
src/Api/appsettings.Development.json: Thanos -> provider=postgres host=pg-dev.internal db=thanos password_in_file=yes
```

**Check the host.** If it points at production, stop and use `--config` to select a development
settings file instead. `--list` never connects, so it is safe to run first.

If the password shows as `placeholder`, set the environment variable the connection string names,
or set `DB_PASSWORD` to override it:

```bash
export DB_PASSWORD=...
```

Use a **read-only** account. On PostgreSQL:

```sql
CREATE ROLE schema_reader LOGIN PASSWORD '...';
GRANT CONNECT ON DATABASE thanos TO schema_reader;
GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA public TO schema_reader;
GRANT pg_read_all_stats TO schema_reader;   -- needed for row counts
```

## Step 4 — Generate the snapshot

```bash
python scripts/dump_schema.py
# Connected: provider=postgres host=pg-dev.internal database=thanos
# Wrote OKF bundle to docs/db/postgres/ (100 tables, 50 views, 50 routines)
```

Narrow it if the database is large:

```bash
python scripts/dump_schema.py --objects trades,trade_leg,vw_position
```

Run `ANALYZE;` first if statistics may be stale — the row counts drive the performance decision, and
stale numbers produce confident wrong answers.

## Step 5 — Decide whether the snapshot is committed

Committing it means the agent finds the schema with no database access at all, and schema drift shows
up in pull requests as a per-table diff — one file changed per altered object, which reviews well.

Committing is usually right. If table names themselves are sensitive, gitignore `docs/db/` and have
each developer generate their own bundle.

## Step 6 — Enable the terminal tool in VS Code

The agent's frontmatter lists `runCommands`. If the agent cannot run the script, open
`story2spec.md`, click **Configure Tools** in the Chat view, and tick the terminal tool — VS Code
writes the correct tool name for your version. A tool name that does not resolve is ignored
silently, which is why this fails quietly rather than erroring.

Leave approvals at the default at first, so you see each command before it runs.

## Step 7 — Run it

Open Copilot Chat, pick **story2spec** from the agents dropdown, and give it a story.

---

## Worked example

**The story**

> **US-4821 — Position summary by book**
> As a trader I want a summary of my positions grouped by book, showing net quantity, average price
> and unrealised P&L, filtered by as-of date, so I can see exposure at a glance.
> AC1: Endpoint `GET /api/positions/summary?bookId=&asOf=`
> AC2: Returns net quantity, average price, unrealised P&L per instrument
> AC3: Responds within 500 ms for a book with 50,000 trades

**What the agent does**

*Phase 1* — reads the story. No work-item tool, so it asks you to paste linked items if AC references
them.

*Phase 2* — declares scope before opening anything:

```
Primary scope:   src/Thanos.Api (endpoint), src/Thanos.Domain/Positions (query logic)
Secondary:       src/Thanos.Data/ThanosDbContext.cs — read only, to confirm mapped entities
Scope mapping:   "position summary" -> Positions feature folder; "book" -> Book entity
```

Then searches for `Position` and `Book`, opens only the located line ranges.

*Phase 2b* — the story needs `vw_position_valuation`, which is not in the DbContext. It runs:

```bash
python scripts/dump_schema.py --list
python scripts/dump_schema.py --objects trades,vw_position_valuation,books
```

then navigates the bundle: `docs/db/postgres/index.md` → `sales/index.md` →
`sales/views/index.md` → `sales/views/vw_position_valuation.md`, follows the foreign-key link from
`sales/tables/trades.md` to `sales/tables/books.md`, and checks `sales/routines/index.md` for an
existing function covering the same job. Four concept files out of 200 — not the whole bundle.

`tables/trades.md` reads:

```markdown
---
type: "PostgreSQL Table"
title: "trades"
description: "Table public.trades in thanos."
tags: [postgres, public, schema]
timestamp: 2026-08-19T06:10:00Z
---

# public.trades

Approximate rows: **4180000** (statistics last updated 2026-08-19)

## Indexes
| index | detail1 |
|---|---|
| idx_trades_book_date | btree (book_id, trade_date) |

## Keys and joins
| type | constraint | column | references |
|---|---|---|---|
| FOREIGN KEY | fk_trades_book | book_id | [books](../tables/books.md) |
```

*The decision* — it works through §4 of `db-design.md`:

- Step 1, hard constraints: single provider, view is owned by the platform team → no schema change proposed.
- Step 2, classify: read-only; 2 tables + 1 view; aggregation already done inside the view; single statement; hot path; 4.18M rows in `trades` but the view filters by `book_id` + `as_of`, which `idx_trades_book_date` covers; expected result ~50 rows.
- Step 3, table: the aggregation lives in the view, so the application query is a filtered read over a keyless entity — **LINQ**.
- Step 4, evidence gate: it has row counts and index coverage from the snapshot, so the recommendation is validated rather than asserted.
- Step 5: raw SQL rejected (the view already handles the joins); stored procedure rejected (single read, no multi-statement transaction, logic not shared).

**What lands in `designs/US-4821/design.md`**

```markdown
Status: READY

## 7. Data access

Provider: PostgreSQL 15. Single provider.

Objects:
| Object | Kind | Resolved from |
|---|---|---|
| [trades](../../docs/db/postgres/tables/trades.md) | table | DbContext (Trade) |
| [books](../../docs/db/postgres/tables/books.md) | table | DbContext (Book) |
| [vw_position_valuation](../../docs/db/postgres/views/vw_position_valuation.md) | view | OKF bundle — NOT in DbContext |

Gap: map vw_position_valuation as a keyless entity (HasNoKey) in ThanosDbContext.
Read-only, no migration required. Owned by the platform team — do not alter.

Approach: EF Core LINQ over the keyless entity.
Justification: decision table row 1 — single read, aggregation already performed in
the view, ~50 rows returned.

Evidence: trades ≈ 4.18M rows; idx_trades_book_date (book_id, trade_date) covers the
view's filter; result set ~50 rows for a typical book. Validated against the OKF bundle
generated 2026-08-19.

Alternatives rejected:
- Raw SQL — the view already encapsulates the joins; no CTE or window function needed.
- Stored procedure — single SELECT, no multi-statement transaction, not shared with
  other systems. Would add deployment and versioning cost for no gain.

Query shape:
    db.PositionValuations
      .AsNoTracking()
      .Where(p => p.BookId == bookId && p.AsOf == asOf)
      .Select(p => new PositionSummaryDto(p.InstrumentId, p.NetQty, p.AvgPrice, p.UnrealisedPnl))
      .ToListAsync(ct)

Transactions: none — read-only path.

Test plan: unit test the projection with an in-memory list; integration test against a
seeded PostgreSQL container asserting AC3 (< 500 ms with 50k trades in scope).
```

**Contrast — the same story without the snapshot**

The agent cannot resolve `vw_position_valuation`. It stops and asks:

> I can't resolve `vw_position_valuation` from the DbContext, migrations, or the OKF bundle, and
> `scripts/dump_schema.py` reports no connection string. Please either run the dump script, or give
> me the view's columns, the underlying tables, their indexes, and approximate row counts.

Running unattended, it writes the document with `Status: BLOCKED` and that question under Open
Questions instead of inventing columns.

---

## If something does not work

| Symptom | Cause |
|---|---|
| Agent not in the dropdown | File not under `.github/agents/`, or workspace not trusted |
| Agent never runs the script | Terminal tool not resolving — use **Configure Tools** |
| `Multiple connection strings found` | Pass `--name <ConnectionName>` |
| `Driver not installed` | Run the `pip install` line the script printed |
| Row counts all zero | Statistics stale (`ANALYZE`) or missing `pg_read_all_stats` |
| Agent invents tables | Bundle missing and it was run unattended — check for `Status: BLOCKED` |
